{"id":"TRAP-003","old_symbol":null,"new_symbol":null,"owner":null,"symbol_kind":"trap","change":"trap","rule_kind":"verifier_visible_but_no_rule_guarantee","match_tokens":[],"trigger":{"match_scope":["*.gdshader","*.tres(shader_type)"],"error_source":"godot --headless --import"},"since_version":null,"since_version_code":0,"until_version":null,"until_version_code":null,"detection_method":"agent_retrieval_or_escalate","semantic_risk":false,"converter_gap":false,"verifier_blind":false,"agent_action":"escalate_human","system_action":null,"warning":"Shader errors are not silent and enter the agent loop like any other verify error. The agent looks up A/B-layer rules by symbol. If a recorded shader migration rule is found (from the shaders_renames cpp array, the Rendering sections of upgrading_to_godot_4.x.rst, or the Updating-shaders carve-out from upgrading_to_godot_4.rst), fix as usual. If no rule is found, do not let the agent guess at a shader fix; escalate directly to needs_human_review.","snippet":"Do not index the Godot engine's C++ source. The error reflects a change in the shader-language spec, not an engine-implementation detail; indexing C++ source is high cost and low signal for this. This is a different concern from \"should shader data itself be indexed\" — shader rename/rewrite data is indexed normally, same as any other symbol_kind. Shader knowledge coverage depends entirely on how much of the official docs/renames tables were parsed into the A/B layers; a miss here is a knowledge-base coverage gap signal, not a reason for the agent to try harder.","source":"manual_trap","source_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/63673","confidence":"verified","payload":{}}
